<template>
  <div class="rag-story">
    <h1 data-scroll-reveal>A Structure-Preserving RAG Ingestion Pipeline</h1>

    <article data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="110">
      <p class="lede">
        At PwC, I helped design the document-processing architecture behind a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)
        system for public enquiries. Its source PDFs contained rules whose meaning depended on tables, notes, and
        decision paths spread across several pages. I focused on the knowledge-base ingestion pipeline, where those
        documents become searchable chunks. Each rule needed to remain connected to the conditions and qualifications
        required to answer a question correctly.
      </p>
      <p>
        Standard PDF parsing could break those connections. It might split a table or paragraph at a page boundary,
        detach a Note or Remark from the sentence it qualified, or leave a flowchart as an image whose decision logic
        could not be searched. Once that structure disappeared, retrieval could return a relevant-looking passage
        without the condition needed to interpret it correctly.
      </p>
      <p>
        I designed a structure-preserving ingestion architecture with a different treatment for each kind of content.
        MinerU preserved structured text and tables. A rule-based linker reattached cross-page notes to the sentences
        they qualified. A visual-language model (VLM) reconstructed flowcharts as Mermaid, a text-based diagram format
        that kept their nodes, labels, and branches searchable. These paths converged before chunking, so RAGFlow
        received consistent units of evidence for embedding, retrieval, and answer generation.
      </p>

      <h2>An architecture shaped by the documents</h2>
      <p>
        The architecture assigns each structural problem to the simplest method that can preserve its meaning
        reliably. A structured parser is well suited to text and table layout. Deterministic linking works for explicit
        references such as Note (4), where the expected relationship can be verified. A VLM is useful when meaning
        lives in the visual connections between flowchart nodes. This separation keeps each stage understandable and
        makes failures easier to locate.
      </p>
      <p>
        The client&rsquo;s operating environment shaped the design as well. The architecture had to fit its existing
        infrastructure and remain affordable and maintainable after implementation. The team preferred to operate a
        single AI model where possible, so I reserved the VLM for flowcharts, where visual understanding provided a
        clear benefit. Text, tables, and cross-page references stayed on more predictable processing paths.
      </p>
      <p>
        The resulting pipeline preserves meaning before retrieval and limits model usage to the stage that needs it.
        I turned that architecture into a client recommendation supported by proof-of-concept evidence, infrastructure
        and cost analysis, technical tradeoffs, and a practical path to implementation.
      </p>

      <div class="stage-list" data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="120">
        <div
