        {
          key: 'mineru',
          name: 'PDF parsing with MinerU',
          body: 'MinerU was the structured PDF parser at the start of the ingestion pipeline. In the test document, it preserved cells spanning multiple rows or columns, reconnected a table divided by a page boundary, and restored a paragraph interrupted by a page break. That produced structured text and tables for the custom repairs that followed.'
        },
        {
          key: 'notes',
          name: 'Cross-page reference linking',
          body: 'Some sentences ended with a reference such as (4), while the corresponding Note (4) appeared several pages later. I wrote a rule-based linker that located the matching note, repaired cases where the parser had split it, and copied it beside the sentence that cited it. The standalone copy was then removed so chunking received one complete rule instead of two disconnected fragments.',
          rules: [
            'Detect a citation such as (4) at the end of a sentence.',
            'Locate the matching Note (4) or Remark (4) elsewhere in the relevant section.',
            'Repair cases where the parser has divided one note into multiple fragments.',
            'Normalise small inconsistencies in the parsed note labels.',
            'Copy the note beside the sentence that cites it, then remove the duplicated standalone copy.'
          ]
        },
        {
          key: 'mermaid',
          name: 'Flowchart reconstruction with VLM and Mermaid',
          body: 'Flowcharts contained decision logic that OCR alone could not preserve because text without edges loses the branch structure. I prompted a VLM to reconstruct each diagram as Mermaid, a text-based diagram format that records nodes, edges, labels, and branches. The Mermaid source could be embedded and retrieved as text while also being rendered back into a diagram for readers.'
        },
        {
          key: 'chunking',
          name: 'Chunking into self-contained retrieval units',
          body: 'RAGFlow then chunked the repaired document into the units returned by retrieval. Because cross-page notes, tables, and paragraphs were repaired first, a chunk could contain both a rule and the qualification needed to interpret it. The goal was a self-contained retrieval unit that could support an answer without relying on a disconnected fragment elsewhere.'
        },
        {
          key: 'embedding',
          name: 'Embedding for semantic matching',
          body: 'Each chunk was converted into a numerical vector representation called an embedding. The query was embedded with the same model, allowing the system to match questions and chunks by semantic similarity even when their wording differed. Mermaid diagrams entered this stage as text, so their decision logic could be retrieved alongside paragraphs and tables.'
        },
        {
          key: 'retrieval',
          name: 'Retrieval of relevant evidence',
          body: 'The system compared the query embedding with the document-chunk embeddings and returned the closest matches. In the controlled test, the chunk containing both the sentence and its linked note became the top result for the question it answered. This provided an end-to-end check that the earlier structural repairs survived into retrieval.'
        },
        {
          key: 'generation',
          name: 'Generation from retrieved evidence',
          body: 'The retrieved chunks were passed to DeepSeek V4-flash to draft an answer grounded in the selected evidence. By this point, each chunk already carried the relevant rule and qualification, giving the generator a more complete basis for its response.'
        }
      ],
      research: [
        {
          title: 'Multi-page OCR with Baidu',
          body: 'I evaluated Baidu Unlimited OCR because its multi-page mode appeared capable of preserving relationships across pages. On difficult public government documents, it preserved page-level layout reasonably well, but the cross-page links required for these questions still needed an explicit linking step. Its multi-page mode also used a lower-detail configuration, and the available description indicated that its training data joined individual pages together.'
        },
        {
          title: 'Embedding pages as images',
          body: 'I also tested image-based and multimodal alternatives. Embedding each page independently still left cross-page notes disconnected from the sentences that cited them. In the RAGFlow path I evaluated, a vision model first converted each image to text, which was then embedded. The system did not embed the page image directly. Reconstructing the relationships explicitly in text produced the most controllable result for these documents.'
        },
        {
          title: 'Three ways to make flowcharts searchable',
          body: 'I tested three ways to process each flowchart. OCR captured the visible text while losing edges and reading order. Free-form VLM descriptions varied from one figure to another. A structured prompt that produced Mermaid preserved every node, edge, and label and answered both test questions consistently.'
        },
        {
          title: 'What the tests changed in the design',
          body: 'Three design decisions followed from the tests. Tables and paragraphs are repaired before chunking because later stages cannot recover structure that has already been split incorrectly. Repeated note numbers are matched using position and surrounding context. Each final chunk carries both the rule and the note that limits or explains it, so retrieval receives complete evidence.'
        }
      ]
    };
  },
};
</script>

